18: Abstract
19: This study develops a label-free digital surrogate assay for cancer risk classification using differential pulse voltammetry (DPV) data. Instead of standard blood biomarkers, 200 electrical current readings per patient serve as input features. Six models from different algorithm families, including sequence-aware 1D-CNN and BiLSTM, were trained on DPV scans from 1,000 patients. The risk label was resulting from the clinical PSA cutoff of 4,000 pg·mL⁻¹, yielding about 11.9% high-risk and 88.1% low-risk patients. A stratified 5-fold cross-validation with localized scaling was used for evaluation. All models delivered consistent, strong results. The classical models reached ROC-AUC values above 99%, and validation accuracies remained above 96%. Logistic Regression performed best with a ROC-AUC of 99.75%, a PR-AUC of 98.17%, and an F1-score of 91.20%. The models diverse in their precision-recall balance, making some more suitable for broad screening and others for targeted confirmatory testing. Logistic Regression caught the most high-risk cases at 95.8% recall, while Random Forest produced the fewest false alarms at 92.3% precision. An ensemble combining all models could provide the most balanced predictions across different clinical settings. These results show that a raw DPV voltammogram, combined with machine learning, can act as a digital surrogate assay that replicates the information content of a multi-biomarker laboratory panel instantly and at low cost, supporting decentralized point-of-care screening.
